1 The Ouestion 


The Kāpālikas or Skull-Bearers, who formed the third group of the Atimārga, 
alongside the Pasupatas and the Lākulas,! were perhaps the most notorious 
Saiva ascetics of classical India. They were known for their cremation ground 
rituals and for wandering around with a skull for an alms bowl. The skull 
(kapāla), their most conspicuous attribute, also provided their name. But the 
Kāpālikas are also designated as Somasiddhāntins, "Those of the Soma Doc- 
trine,”? or the “Soma People with the Skull"? These appellations seem to have 
been of some importance because their initiation names also included or 
ended in -soma in most cases (e.g. Satyasoma, Devasomā,* Somibhattāraka*). 
What was this Somasiddhanta, doctrine of Soma orteaching about (the) Soma? 


