himself, in Visvaksena or Dattātreya, in Visņu-Nārāyaņa or his weapons. It is 
also equally clear that from early on the Srivaisnava tradition was united in 
seeing Rāmānuja as central to the salvific process, and in considering that, for 
those who were his disciples, taking refuge in Ramanuja would accelerate the 
path to Vaikuntha and the state of servitude (kainkarya) to Visnu-Narayana. 
Nevertheless, the frequent references to Ramanuja as destroying one’s sins, a 
reference directly calqued on the central promise of the Caramasloka, is not 
absent but does not function as the fulcrum of the vs as it does in the 1N. 

Here, we would do well to recollect Vedanta Dešika's poetic tribute to other 
figures of the guruparampara in the kavya style, such as his references to Nam- 
